/**
 * Bundled city -> coordinate dataset (ticket e5e1aa1), replacing
 * `METRO_AREA_GROUPS`' two hand-curated metro tables with a nationwide
 * lookup that `metroAreas.ts` uses to compute real distance instead of set
 * membership.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * PROVENANCE
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * Source: the U.S. Census Bureau's 2023 Gazetteer Files, national Places
 * file -- https://www2.census.gov/geo/docs/maps-data/data/gazetteer/2023_Gazetteer/2023_Gaz_place_national.zip
 * (downloaded 2026-10-10; last-modified per the server: 2023-09-26). Free,
 * public domain (U.S. government work), and the same authority class the
 * deleted `METRO_AREA_GROUPS` table already held itself to (it cited
 * OMB/Census CBSA numbers) -- this is the Census Bureau's OWN place-level
 * data, one level down from the metro-area (CBSA) product that table used.
 * `INTPTLAT`/`INTPTLONG` -- each place's internal point, not a street
 * address or city-hall pin -- are the lat/lon pulled from it unmodified
 * (rounded to 6 decimal places, sub-meter precision, no information lost
 * that matters here). `ALAND_SQMI` (land area, square miles, rounded to 3
 * decimal places) is carried through too -- NOT used by resolution (there is
 * no honest population-based tie-break available here; see
 * `resolveCallerCities`'s doc comment for why land area was tried and
 * rejected as a resolution heuristic), only to pick which state to name in
 * an ambiguous-name warning's example text, where a wrong guess costs
 * nothing worse than a slightly-off hint.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * FILTERING APPLIED (2026-10-10, re-verified after e5e1aa1 review round 2)
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 *  - Census Designated Places (LSAD "57" -- unincorporated, statistical-only
 *    boundaries) EXCLUDED EVERYWHERE EXCEPT HAWAII: 12,360 rows. A real job
 *    posting's location field names the place a company's office is
 *    legally/colloquially in ("Bellevue, WA"), not a CDP boundary ("East
 *    Renton Highlands CDP"); keeping them would have more than doubled the
 *    within-radius candidate count for a dense metro (measured: 273
 *    unfiltered WA places within 60 miles of Seattle before this exclusion,
 *    98 after).
 *
 *    HAWAII IS THE EXCEPTION, AND MUST BE: Hawaii has NO incorporated
 *    municipalities at all -- every one of its 163 Gazetteer rows is a CDP
 *    (verified 2026-10-10 against the raw file's own LSAD column). The CDP
 *    exclusion above, applied uniformly, deleted the entire state --
 *    "Honolulu" resolved to nothing, with no state suffix able to rescue it,
 *    which is a worse version of the exact bug this ticket exists to fix
 *    (review round 2, D1). So Hawaii's CDPs are kept, and this is the ONE
 *    state where the coverage-cost claim above does not hold -- it was
 *    costing 100% of a top-50-by-population city, not a hypothetical. Also
 *    checked and confirmed FINE: Alaska has 149 non-CDP rows (145 city +
 *    1 municipality + 3 city-and-borough), so it needed no such exception;
 *    its own real bug was a name-normalization one, covered next.
 *
 *    One further Hawaii wrinkle: the Census Bureau's own Gazetteer renamed
 *    the historic "Honolulu" CDP to "Urban Honolulu" (to distinguish it from
 *    the City & County of Honolulu, which spans all of Oahu) -- so even with
 *    CDPs kept, a bare "Honolulu" would still resolve to nothing under the
 *    name that's actually in the file. One alias row (`HI|honolulu|...`,
 *    same coordinates as `urban honolulu`) is appended after the sorted body
 *    below specifically to undo that renaming for this dataset's purposes.
 *
 *  - Rows outside the 50 states + DC EXCLUDED: 292 (Puerto Rico only --
 *    verified 2026-10-10 that Guam, American Samoa, the Northern Mariana
 *    Islands and the U.S. Virgin Islands appear in the raw file ONLY as
 *    CDPs, so the CDP exclusion above already removes them; none of the 292
 *    excluded here is a region `REGION_CODE_BY_NAME` recognizes anyway).
 *  - Each name had its LSAD-code-specific descriptor stripped by WORD COUNT,
 *    keyed off the Gazetteer's own LSAD column rather than guessed from the
 *    trailing text (review round 2, D2): LSAD "25" always strips exactly one
 *    trailing word, "city"; LSAD "53" strips exactly three, "city and
 *    borough"; and so on for every LSAD code this file contains (borough,
 *    town, village, metro township, municipality, corporation, and the
 *    unified/consolidated/metropolitan-government family) -- see
 *    `scripts/` history / the ticket for the exact per-code table. This
 *    replaced an earlier version that stripped only the LAST WORD
 *    regardless of how many words the real descriptor had, which silently
 *    corrupted exactly two real names into a DIFFERENT real place:
 *    "Juneau city and borough" (AK) lost only "borough", leaving "Juneau
 *    city and" -- not a key in this file at all, so a nationally-ambiguous
 *    name (Juneau, AK is also Juneau, WI) looked FALSELY UNAMBIGUOUS, and
 *    "include nearby cities" for "Juneau" would have silently expanded to
 *    Wisconsin. "Pearl City" (HI) had the same issue once Hawaii's CDPs were
 *    added back (above) interacting with the single-word stripper. Both are
 *    fixed by the same per-LSAD-word-count rule; neither was a hypothetical,
 *    and the per-LSAD approach is what makes a NEW instance of the same bug
 *    structurally impossible rather than merely patched for these two names.
 *  - A small family of consolidated city-county governments (LSAD "00" --
 *    no fixed descriptor text, "unified government", "consolidated
 *    government", "urban county" and "metropolitan government"/"metro
 *    government") has its OWN descriptor stripped the same way, and then --
 *    ONLY for this LSAD family, never for an ordinary city/town's own
 *    hyphenated name -- a hyphenated remainder's first segment is kept as
 *    the recognizable principal city: "Nashville-Davidson" -> "nashville",
 *    "Lexington-Fayette" (LSAD "urban county") -> "lexington",
 *    "Macon-Bibb County" -> "macon", "Athens-Clarke County unified
 *    government" -> "athens", "Augusta-Richmond County consolidated
 *    government" -> "augusta", "Anaconda-Deer Lodge County" -> "anaconda".
 *    All six were unreachable (a real top-100-city no-op each) before this
 *    fix. An ordinary place whose OWN name has a hyphen ("Winston-Salem
 *    city", LSAD "25") is never touched by this rule -- only the five
 *    consolidated-government LSAD codes are -- so it is not newly at risk of
 *    being chopped to "Winston".
 *  - 8 rows REJECTED outright after normalization, because the result still
 *    contained a character this file's lookup can't safely anchor a
 *    `\b...\b` word-boundary pattern on (a diacritic, a slash, or a
 *    comma): Utqiagvik AK, La Canada Flintridge CA, Canon City CO,
 *    Islamorada FL, the Louisville/Jefferson County and Hartsville/
 *    Trousdale County consolidated city-counties (KY, TN -- note plain
 *    "Louisville, KY" resolves FINE despite this, via its own separate
 *    "Louisville city" row; Jefferson County's consolidated-government row
 *    is simply not the only way to reach it), Espanola NM, and the
 *    Lynchburg/Moore County consolidated government (TN). Each is a real,
 *    known coverage gap -- "include nearby cities" cannot resolve a phrase
 *    naming one of these eight -- recorded here rather than silently
 *    dropped.
 *  - Of the 21 cases where normalization produced the exact same (state,
 *    name) pair twice (e.g. two historically-distinct "Oakwood"s in OH, or
 *    Hawaii's own "Kailua" and "Waimea" CDPs, each real in two different
 *    places on different islands), ALL rows are kept, not deduped --
 *    `nearbyCitiesWithinRadius` unions every coordinate for a name, so a
 *    phrase near either one is found.
 *
 * Result: 19,670 rows (19,669 from the Gazetteer file plus the one Honolulu
 * alias above), the first 19,669 sorted by (state, name, lat, lon) for a
 * reviewable diff, the alias appended after. All 51 state codes (50 states
 * + DC) are present -- asserted by `metroAreas.test.ts`, which is what would
 * have caught the Hawaii gap immediately rather than needing a live report.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * FORMAT
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * One row per line, `|`-delimited (not a JS array literal -- 19,670 tuple
 * literals would be slow for Prettier and tsc to no benefit): USPS state
 * code, lowercase place name, latitude, longitude, land area in square
 * miles. Parsed once at module load into `CITY_COORDINATES` below, with a
 * field-count and finite-number check on every row -- a malformed row
 * throws at module load (loud and immediate) rather than silently producing
 * `NaN` coordinates that would only surface later as a mysteriously-always-
 * false distance comparison.
 *
 * This file is excluded from Prettier (`.prettierignore`) -- not merely
 * untouched by it today because a single 700KB-plus line happens to already
 * satisfy `prettier --check`. That was true by accident, not by exclusion,
 * until this line was added; a future edit that reformats this file's
 * single string literal onto multiple lines would still pass `prettier
 * --check` today with no exclusion, which is the wrong invariant to depend
 * on for a verbatim derived-from-source data table (the same reasoning
 * `.prettierignore` already applies to the Lever/Greenhouse fixture
 * captures).
 */

interface RawCityCoordinate {
  readonly state: string;
  readonly name: string;
  readonly lat: number;
  readonly lon: number;
  /** Land area, square miles -- see this file's header for what it is (and
   * is not) used for. */
  readonly alandSqMi: number;
}

